### A3. Barrier Check, Determinant/Height Ledger, and "2/3 a priori"

1. **Determinant / Arakelov height ledger (`PROVED`):**
   Bost's slope inequality (`SlopeInequality` / `MuMaxLeMin` in [LatticeSlopes/Statement.lean](file:///private/tmp/claude-501/-Volumes-git/c1686473-142d-57cc-8202-223c6b26715f/scratchpad/leansrc/lean/src/Formalism/LatticeSlopes/Statement.lean#L75-L103), where $\mu(L) = -\frac{1}{\operatorname{rk} L}\log\operatorname{covol}(L)$) **is** Minkowski's determinant bound in logarithmic coordinates (cf. CHHS capacity theory in [topblock_results.md](file:///Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/topblock_results.md#L351-L359)):

   | Ledger item | Coppersmith / Howgrave-Graham ($p = N^\beta, |x_0| \le N^\xi$) | RH-remark Bost-slope ledger (`TypeParams` in [StatementOCCM.lean](file:///private/tmp/claude-501/-Volumes-git/c1686473-142d-57cc-8202-223c6b26715f/scratchpad/leansrc/lean/src/Formalism/ProductFormula/StatementOCCM.lean#L37-L105)) |
   |---|---|---|
   | **$p$-adic gain (LHS)** | $m\log p = m\beta\log N$ at **one** hidden root $x_0$ | $\sum_{p \le X\text{ inert}} m\bigl(\frac{a}{p}-w\bigr)_+\log p$ across **all** inert $p \le X$ |
   | **Archimedean + covolume cost (RHS)** | $\frac{1}{d}\log\det L = \bigl(\frac{m(m+1)}{2d} + \frac{d-1}{2}\xi\bigr)\log N$ ([topblock_results.md](file:///Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/topblock_results.md#L310-L321) Lemma MI; [codex_r8_copp_bridge.md](file:///Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/codex_r8_copp_bridge.md#L50-L75)) | $m(a\log a - a - a\log\Omega) - a\log T + \text{covol} \approx m b X \log X$ (`MAIN` in [DataParams₂.lean](file:///private/tmp/claude-501/-Volumes-git/c1686473-142d-57cc-8202-223c6b26715f/scratchpad/leansrc/lean/src/Formalism/LocalRingCardinality/DataParams₂.lean#L232-L235)) |
   | **Single-prime balance** | $\xi < 2\beta\theta - \theta^2 \le \beta^2 = \frac{1}{4}$ ($n/4$ bits needed; cofactor $q^{m-j}$ eats half the modulus) | One prime $p$ gives $\frac{ma}{p}\log p$ vs. archimedean $ma\log a$: ratio $\frac{\log p}{p\log a} \ll N^{-1/2} < 1$ |

   *Why neither beats $N^{1/4}$:* $D^a\theta(0)$ is **modulus-blind**, so its archimedean height $a\log a$ pays for *all* inert primes $\le a$ simultaneously; a single factor $p \mid N$ accounts for only $O(\frac{\log p}{p\log a})$ of its height, so $E_a$ never vanishes over $\mathbb{Z}$ from $p$-divisibility. Conversely, any polynomial/section that *does* inject $N$ via $(N, x+p_0)^m$ pays the cofactor $q^{m-j}$ covolume $\frac{m(m+1)}{2}\log N$, hitting Lemma MI's $\beta^2 = 1/4$ barrier identically.

2. **What "stop at 2/3 a priori" actually means (`PROVED` from Lean source):**
   In [GrowthBracketBoundOfSizeWindows.lean](file:///private/tmp/claude-501/-Volumes-git/c1686473-142d-57cc-8202-223c6b26715f/scratchpad/leansrc/lean/src/Formalism/SizeWindowBrackets/GrowthBracketBoundOfSizeWindows.lean#L104-L108) (`bracketSizeWindowBrackets`) and [SChain.lean](file:///private/tmp/claude-501/-Volumes-git/c1686473-142d-57cc-8202-223c6b26715f/scratchpad/leansrc/lean/src/Formalism/SizeWindowBrackets/SChain.lean#L207-L225) (`SizesTextAt`), the bound on $G_z(X)$ after $m$ lifts from initial unitary rank $k$ averaged over an $n$-character Iwasawa tower is (up to $X^{O(\varepsilon)}$):
   $$\text{Error}(X) \;\asymp\; \frac{X}{m} \;+\; \frac{X m}{k} \;+\; m \;+\; \frac{k}{n^2}.$$
   - **A priori ($n = O(1)$, before Iwasawa-tower twisting / "division"):** Minimizing $\frac{X}{m} + \frac{Xm}{k} + m + k$ forces $\frac{X}{m} = \frac{Xm}{k} = k \implies m = X^{1/3},\; k = X^{2/3}$, giving $G_z(X) \ll X^{2/3+\varepsilon}$ — i.e., **the zero-free region stops at $\operatorname{Re}(s) > 2/3$**.
   - **Crossing $2/3 \to 1/2$:** Gluing across an Iwasawa tower of size $n \asymp X^2$ (`BNBounds` + Behrend sets in [BehrendSets/Statement.lean](file:///private/tmp/claude-501/-Volumes-git/c1686473-142d-57cc-8202-223c6b26715f/scratchpad/leansrc/lean/src/Formalism/BehrendSets/Statement.lean#L48-L62)) divides $k$ by $n^2$; setting $m \asymp X^{1/2}$ and $k \asymp X^{9/2}$ makes all four terms $\ll X^{1/2+\varepsilon}$.
   - This $2/3$ is an **analytic $X^{2/3}$ power** ($L_X[1]$), **not** subexponential complexity $L_N[2/3]$.

